        <section class="granite-section granite-two-col" aria-labelledby="granite-example-title">
          <div>
            <p class="granite-kicker">Check the math</p><h2 id="granite-example-title">A 50 × 12 ft driveway example</h2>
            <p>Suppose the measured surface area is 600 sq ft and the planned finished granite layer is 3 in deep. This describes that layer only. It does not include a separate base or excavation volume.</p>
            <ol class="granite-steps">
              <li><strong>Finished volume:</strong> 600 × 3 ÷ 324 = 5.56 cu yd (150 cu ft).</li>
              <li><strong>Weight example:</strong> 5.56 × 2,800 ÷ 2,000 = 7.78 US short tons.</li>
              <li><strong>If 10% extra is justified:</strong> 5.56 × 1.10 = 6.11 cu yd, or about 8.56 tons at that same weight.</li>
            </ol>
            <p>One cubic yard covers 108 sq ft at 3 in deep by geometry. At 2,800 lb/cu yd, one US short ton represents about 77 sq ft at that depth. Replace 2,800 with your supplier’s figure before converting a yard quote into tons.</p>
          </div>
          <aside class="granite-note">
            <h3>Before calling the yard</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>Ask whether the product is <strong>clean</strong>, <strong>minus</strong>, or another graded mix.</li>
              <li>Confirm the nominal size and loose pounds per cubic yard.</li>
              <li>Measure each planned layer separately and check site drainage.</li>
              <li>Ask how the seller prices, rounds, and delivers the order.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>A truck’s legal payload and a quarry scale ticket depend on the actual vehicle and delivered material. The calculator does not assume a fixed truckload.</p>
          </aside>
        </section>

        <section class="granite-section granite-sources" aria-labelledby="granite-sources-title">
          <h2 id="granite-sources-title">How the assumptions were checked</h2>
          <p><a href="https://landcarestone.com/sand-gravel/3-4-crushed-stone-2/" rel="noopener noreferrer">LandCare Stone lists 3/4 in crushed granite at 2,800 lb per cubic yard</a>; <a href="https://www.landscapecentre.com/products/bulk-materials/gravel" rel="noopener noreferrer">Landscape Centre lists clear-crush granite at about 2,600 lb per cubic yard</a>. Those are supplier examples, not a universal density. <a href="https://store.astm.org/c0029_c0029m-23.html" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASTM C29/C29M</a> describes a method for measuring bulk density and voids. LandCare also suggests about 100 sq ft per cubic yard at 3 in; the calculator's 108 sq ft is pure volume geometry. Its smaller listed coverage is a more conservative buying guide, so confirm the seller's recommendation for your job.</p>
          <p>The <a href="https://www.fhwa.dot.gov/construction/pubs/ots15002.pdf" rel="noopener noreferrer">FHWA gravel-roads guide</a> explains why a road surface needs an appropriate blend of coarse stone and smaller particles, while the <a href="https://www.epa.gov/sites/default/files/2021-04/documents/green_streets_design_manual_feb_2021_web_res_small_508.pdf" rel="noopener noreferrer">EPA Green Streets Handbook</a> describes clean open-graded aggregate for drainage reservoirs. The appropriate section and layer depth depend on site and design.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="granite-section granite-faq" aria-labelledby="granite-faq-title">
          <h2 id="granite-faq-title">Crushed granite questions</h2>
          <div class="granite-faq-grid">{faqItems.map(item => <div><h3>{item.q}</h3><p>{item.a}</p></div>)}</div>
